    "description": "A bakhshdari is the administration of a bakhsh, the territorial district beneath an Iranian county. Its appointed district head operates within a framework that also includes rural districts, village administrations, and local councils.",
    "tags": ["District administration", "Bakhshdari", "Rural governance", "Territorial divisions"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:48:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:48:09Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "District Administrations in Iran: Bakhsh and Bakhshdari",
      "description": "Learn where Iran's district administrations fit, how district heads are appointed, and how rural coordination differs from council authority."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-of-district",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an Iranian district contains",
        "content": "The Persian term **bakhsh** means a territorial district beneath a county, or **shahrestan**. Its administration is the **bakhshdari**, headed by a **bakhshdar**. A **dehestan**, usually translated as rural district, is a different, subordinate territorial unit. Article 12 of the national divisions law establishes this hierarchy. [s1][s2]\n\nArticle 6 describes a bakhsh as neighboring rural districts encompassing settlements and potentially towns. Its criteria consider geography, population, accessibility, and social and economic connections. A bakhsh is therefore not simply an urban neighborhood. The law also recognizes geographic differences through provisions addressing remote, border, island, desert, and other sparsely populated areas. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "appointment-and-rural-links",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An appointed office with rural connections",
        "content": "Article 6 of the administrative resolution specifies nomination by the county governor, approval by the provincial governor, and an appointment order from the interior minister. Its accompanying provision allows the minister's authority under that article to be delegated to provincial governors. [s2]\n\nAn official description of rural-affairs duties published by Zanjan's provincial administration illustrates the surrounding institutional network. It assigns the provincial office work on establishing village administrations in cooperation with county and district offices, communicating rural-development objectives, and supporting village administrations with equipment and interagency coordination. These are the provincial office's published responsibilities, rather than a complete national job description for every district head. [s3]\n\nThe same document links rural administration with project monitoring, review of development proposals, and identifying villages exposed to hazards such as floods. It shows why district administration needs to be understood alongside specialist provincial offices and village institutions. It does not establish whether these tasks were completed in a particular locality. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "institutional-map",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Territorial hierarchy around a district",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Central administration\"] -->|\"Administrative hierarchy\"| B[\"Province: ostan\"]\n  B -->|\"Administrative hierarchy\"| C[\"County: shahrestan\"]\n  C -->|\"Administrative hierarchy\"| D[\"District: bakhsh\"]\n  D -->|\"Administrative hierarchy\"| E[\"Rural district: dehestan\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show territorial administrative subordination under Article 12. They do not describe council representation, funding, or appointment procedures.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "council-boundary",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "District administration and local representation",
        "content": "The district head and the district council are distinct institutions. Article 103 of the Constitution expressly includes district heads among appointed officials required to respect council decisions within the councils' authority. Article 105 also places council decisions within the constraints of national law. [s4]\n\nThese provisions make the legal boundaries important: an appointment does not eliminate council powers, while a council decision must remain within its lawful scope. For a specific development project, the meaningful questions are which institution approved it, which agency implements it, and what evidence documents progress. An organizational title alone cannot answer those questions. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
