    "description": "Iran's disaster-management framework combines a national coordinating organization with provincial and county structures. Its legal arrangements distinguish policy and coordination, emergency command, participating agencies, and organized volunteer support.",
    "tags": ["Disaster management", "Emergency coordination", "Provincial administration", "Resilience", "Volunteer participation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:48:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:48:09Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Disaster Management in Iran: Provincial and County Organizations",
      "description": "Explore Iran's disaster coordination structures, escalation of emergency command, volunteer arrangements, and performance-evaluation framework."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "organization-and-command",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A system of coordination and emergency command",
        "content": "Iran's Disaster Management Law, adopted in 1398 Solar Hijri, establishes coordination headquarters at national, provincial, and county levels. Article 10 assigns provincial chairmanship to the provincial governor and county chairmanship to the county governor; disaster-management managers serve as their secretaries. The national organization and territorial headquarters are therefore related but distinct parts of the framework. [s1]\n\nArticle 11 specifies escalation according to territorial reach and response capacity. A county governor can declare an emergency when necessary while informing the provincial governor. Where county capacity is exceeded, provincial assumption of command depends on the procedure set out in that article. National escalation likewise requires reporting and confirmation. Tehran city has a separate provision assigning emergency declaration and command to the interior minister. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coordination-structure",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Who chairs the coordination headquarters?",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Interior minister\"] -->|\"Chairs\"| B[\"National headquarters\"]\n  C[\"Provincial governor\"] -->|\"Chairs\"| D[\"Provincial headquarters\"]\n  E[\"County governor\"] -->|\"Chairs\"| F[\"County headquarters\"]\n```",
        "summary": "These arrows show chairmanship under Article 10, not an automatic transfer of emergency command. Article 11 separately governs escalation and includes a special rule for Tehran city.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "organized-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How volunteers and civil organizations fit",
        "content": "A regulation adopted in 1402 Solar Hijri addresses participation by civil organizations, volunteers, and private businesses. It requires provincial disaster-management directorates to implement specified coordination measures across their provinces, including counties and villages. Participating executive agencies retain responsibility for the proper execution of tasks entrusted through these arrangements. [s2]\n\nThe regulation also provides for training, exercises, documentation, and accident-insurance arrangements. During declared emergencies, coordination offices may be established in affected areas when the relevant headquarters chair determines they are needed. These offices connect participating organizations with operational headquarters and the field commander, under the field commander's supervision. [s2]\n\nThe distinction is practical: willingness to help, institutional coordination, and operational command are separate matters. The regulation describes how assistance should enter the response system; its existence alone does not establish how comprehensively volunteers have been registered, trained, insured, or deployed. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-capacity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What would demonstrate preparedness?",
        "content": "A cabinet resolution adopted in 1400 Solar Hijri establishes a performance-monitoring framework. Its indicators address such matters as organizational structures, progress in making buildings and equipment safer, implementation of approved plans, staff training, and the availability of required information. It also provides for annual assessment and reporting. [s3]\n\nThese are prescribed measures, not published results. A defensible comparison between provinces would require actual observations, consistent definitions, and clear reporting periods. The inspected resolution supplies no populated annual dataset from which to rank administrations or chart improving readiness. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recovery-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovery extends beyond the emergency",
        "content": "The government-led, United Nations-supported assessment of Iran's 1398 floods illustrates a different stage: identifying damage, losses, and recovery needs after the immediate response. Its foreword describes a pilot assessment involving government specialists, provincial departments, and United Nations technical support. It frames recovery as a basis for integrated rebuilding and development. [s4]\n\nThat assessment is a historical case, rather than a current audit of every provincial organization. Read alongside the legal framework, it helps distinguish emergency coordination from the longer work of assessing needs and planning reconstruction. Neither a command chart nor a recovery plan, by itself, proves that the intended outcomes were achieved. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      }
    ],
